---
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { PROFILE } from '../data/profile';
import { SITE } from '../consts';
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Copyright and licensing"
  description="Licenses for ORIZ.IN editorial content, source code, brand assets, and third-party material, plus how to report a copyright concern."
  lastUpdated="25 October 2026"
>
  <h2>Editorial content</h2>
  <p>
    Unless a page states otherwise, original articles, guides, and editorial
    explanations are licensed under
    {' '}
    <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)
    </a>. You may copy, redistribute, and adapt the material, including
    commercially, if you give appropriate credit, link to the license, and
    indicate whether changes were made. You may not imply that ORIZ.IN endorses
    you or your use.
  </p>
  <p>Suggested attribution:</p>
  <blockquote>
    “HTTP Status Codes, Explained Without the Guesswork” by Chirag Singhal,
    ORIZ.IN, licensed CC BY 4.0. Source:
    https://oriz.in/technology/backend-apis/http-status-codes/
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Code</h2>
  <p>
    The site’s source code is released under the MIT License. See the
    {' '}
    <a href={SITE.repoUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer">repository</a> and its
    <code>LICENSE</code> file. Dependencies retain their own licenses. Before
    reusing a third-party library, review the version and license in the
    repository’s dependency lockfile.
  </p>

  <h2>Brand</h2>
  <p>
    The ORIZ.IN name, wordmark, logo, and visual identity are used to identify
    this project. They are not licensed for use in a way that suggests
    affiliation, endorsement, or the same source. Do not use the brand to
    distribute modified content as if it were the original site.
  </p>

  <h2>Third-party material</h2>
  <p>
    Articles may quote or link to specifications, documentation, datasets, and
    APIs owned by others. Those materials remain under their respective
    licenses. API entries are summaries and links, not a redistribution of a
    provider’s full documentation. The license field on an API page describes
    the provider’s terms as reviewed, not a license granted by ORIZ.IN.
  </p>
  <p>
    Trademarks and product names belong to their owners. Their appearance here
    is descriptive and does not imply sponsorship.
  </p>

  <h2>User-provided content</h2>
  <p>
    The tools do not upload user content to ORIZ.IN. If you send an email or
    open a public issue, do not include material you do not have the right to
    share. Do not send confidential documents or third-party copyrighted work
    unless you have permission.
  </p>

  <h2>Copyright concerns</h2>
  <p>
    If you believe material on ORIZ.IN infringes your rights, email
    <a href={`mailto:${PROFILE.email}?subject=Copyright%20concern`}>{PROFILE.email}</a>
    with the page URL, the work you own, a description of the concern, and your
    contact details. Legitimate concerns will be investigated promptly; content
    may be removed or replaced while a claim is reviewed.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>
    For licensing questions, use the same email with the subject
    “Licensing”. Related: <a href="/terms/">terms of use</a> and
    <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
